## 12. Erratum — a real bug found and fixed after initial delivery

**What was wrong:** the original `focus_score` function used each AOI's raw share (e.g. 23.2% → 0.232) directly as a pseudo-probability in the entropy formula, without renormalizing so the values summed to 1. Since AOI shares never actually sum to 100% (some attention always lands on unassigned background), this made the score sensitive to **how much total attention got captured by named regions**, not just **how that captured attention was distributed among them**. Two creatives with identical concentration shape but different total-captured-mass could get different, non-comparable scores.

**How it was caught:** while comparing a baseline recharge banner (34.5% of attention captured by AOIs) against a redesign (45.4% captured, zero competition flags, headline dominance jumped from 4.6% to 23.2%), the buggy formula reported the *redesign* as less concentrated (0.467) than the *baseline* (0.518) — directly contradicting the competition-flag count and the per-AOI shares, which both clearly favored the redesign. That inconsistency is what triggered the investigation.

**The fix:** renormalize the AOI shares to sum to 1 before computing entropy, so the score purely measures distribution shape.

**Corrected numbers for that case:**
| | Buggy formula | Corrected formula |
|---|---|---|
| Baseline | 0.518 | **0.115** |
| Redesign | 0.467 | **0.233** |

Under the fix, the redesign is roughly double the concentration of the baseline — consistent with the rest of the evidence.

**What this means for earlier results in this document:** the distortion is largest when comparing two versions with very different total captured-attention mass. Most before/after comparisons in this project had more similar totals between versions, so conclusions likely hold — but this hasn't been individually re-verified for every case, and the honest position is "probably fine, not confirmed" until it is. If precision matters for a specific comparison, re-run it with the patched function rather than trusting the original numbers reported earlier in this document.

**Good interview material, honestly:** finding and fixing a real bug in your own measurement tool — one that could have flipped a real conclusion — is a stronger story than a pipeline that never needed debugging. It's also a direct demonstration of the exact skill this whole project has been about: not trusting a confident-looking number without checking what's actually behind it, including your own numbers.

## 14. Two additions — global-effect (weighted-attention) model and motion salience

**Trigger for both:** a request to explore whether "superposition of attention" — multiple candidate attention targets coexisting before one is selected — has any real, implementable basis, rather than being a decorative metaphor.

### 14a. The global effect / averaging-saccade model (`averaging_saccade_prediction` in `saliency_pipeline.py`)

**The real science:** the "global effect" or "averaging saccade" phenomenon is mainstream, uncontested oculomotor research (Findlay, 1982, and 40+ years of replication — PLOS Biology 2018, Journal of Vision 2013, and many more). When two salient targets sit close together, the eye does not cleanly choose one — it lands at the population-weighted centroid between them, sometimes on neither target.

**What it adds:** the pipeline's existing competition detector could already say "these two AOIs are competing." This adds a sharper, mechanistically distinct claim: an actual predicted (x, y) landing coordinate, and a hard classification of whether that predicted fixation lands on *either* competing element or on empty space between them — a literal "wasted fixation" prediction.

**Honest scope:** the global effect is best-documented for short-latency, *reflexive* saccades specifically — it weakens for slower, deliberate viewing. This is framed as modeling the very first, fastest glance (relevant to fast-scroll social/ad contexts), not the entire viewing sequence.

**What was explicitly rejected before landing on this:** the original prompt that led here was about "superposition of attention" and recent papers on the topic. Real research search turned up *quantum cognition* (Busemeyer & Bruza, 2012; Pothos & Busemeyer in *Proc. Royal Society B* and *Behavioral and Brain Sciences*) — a genuine peer-reviewed field using quantum *probability formalism* (not literal physics) to model context-dependent judgment. But a 2026 *Frontiers in Psychology* critical review was found stating plainly that much of the adjacent literature uses "superposition" as verbal decoration without testable mechanisms. That distinction mattered: the quantum-cognition framing was set aside in favor of the global-effect model above, which achieves the same "multiple candidates held in a weighted state before collapse" idea using mainstream, uncontested vision science instead.

### 14b. Motion salience — Hassenstein-Reichardt correlator (`motion_emd.py`, new file)

**The real science:** the Hassenstein-Reichardt correlator (1956) is a motion-detection model literally reverse-engineered from insect (beetle/fly) motion-perception experiments, still cited today as legitimate front-end architecture for optic-flow computation.

**Trigger:** a viral trend (Sept 2026) of running the newly-published MaleCNS v1.0 fruit-fly connectome (166,700 neurons, full CNS) through toy simulations for unrelated tasks (games, trading). That full-connectome approach was explicitly rejected as a literal input to this pipeline — a fly's CNS is overwhelmingly flight/olfaction/motor circuitry with no analog for text, faces, or brand logos; wiring it in would have been the same fabricated-legitimacy mistake this whole project exists to catch in other tools. The one piece that *does* transfer honestly is the correlator model itself, not the connectome.

**What it adds:** every prior part of this pipeline is static-image only. This extends it to video/animated creative (Reels, animated banners, GIFs) — a real, previously-unaddressed gap, since a moving CTA pulse or animated flourish can dominate attention in ways no single frame can capture.

**Validated:** on a synthetic two-frame test before any real use — a moving dot cleanly separated from a static noisy background (0.00 mean energy on background vs. 15.98 in the motion path), and direction discrimination confirmed to flip sign correctly between opposite motions.

**What was explicitly rejected:** a separate idea (inspired by the "God's Eye View" viral OSINT/geospatial-fusion tool) to build a live competitive-intelligence layer by scraping Meta Ad Library. Tested directly — blocked by robots.txt, same as every other social platform hit in this project. Documented here as a rejected idea, not a working feature: the manual workaround (person pulls competitor screenshots themselves, pipeline analyzes them) is what's actually usable today.

