## 15. v2 static saliency, validated against real human eye-tracking (added Oct 2026)

**What changed.** `saliency_v2.py` replaces the hand-weighted Spectral Residual + Fine-Grained blend with 8 channels (Spectral Residual, Fine-Grained, Frequency-Tuned colour saliency, colour-opponent centre-surround, luminance centre-surround, local contrast energy, multi-face prior, centre prior). Channel weights are fitted by non-negative least squares on a TRAIN split and scored on a HELD-OUT split (OSIE, Xu et al. 2014: 700 images, 15 viewers each; 500 train / 200 test, fixed seed 2026). `validate_osie.py` reproduces everything.

**Result (200 held-out images, mean [95% bootstrap CI]):**

| Model | CC | SIM | KLD (lower better) | NSS | AUC-Judd |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Random noise | 0.000 | 0.267 | 1.966 | 0.011 | 0.507 |
| Centre prior only | 0.297 | 0.357 | 1.345 | 0.821 | 0.735 |
| v1, no face prior | 0.275 | 0.361 | 1.366 | 0.870 | 0.733 |
| v1 (original pipeline) | 0.344 | 0.375 | 1.318 | 1.081 | 0.743 |
| **v2 learned fusion** | **0.450** [0.432, 0.469] | **0.395** | **1.147** | **1.403** | **0.819** |
| Human vs human (7 vs 8 viewers) | 0.883 | 0.732 | 0.615 | 3.509 | 0.933 |

Paired gain v2 minus v1: CC +0.105 [0.089, 0.121], NSS +0.321, AUC +0.076, KLD -0.170. v2 beat v1 on 83% of test images (median per-image CC gain +0.111; 10th percentile -0.054, i.e. it loses on some images).

**Honest reading.**
- This answers Limitation 1 ("no human ground truth") for NATURAL SCENES only. OSIE is free viewing of photographs, not advertising. Weights should be re-fitted on ad-specific eye-tracking data.
- v2 reaches about half of human-to-human agreement. It is a better screening heuristic, not a replacement for a panel.
- A centre prior alone (CC 0.297) matched the original low-level saliency without faces (0.275). Most of v1's lift came from the face prior.
- The learned weights put the most mass on the face prior (0.78), then contrast energy (0.18), colour centre-surround (0.14), Spectral Residual (0.13), centre (0.11). Fine-Grained and luminance centre-surround were fitted to 0. Faces dominate partly because OSIE contains many people; treat that weight as dataset-dependent.
- **Colour / button blind spot (Section 8) re-tested** (`button_test.py`, synthetic stimuli, each analysed alone, button centred, so compare against the 7.2% no-colour-contrast baseline): bordered/flat ratio 0.77 in v1 vs 1.03 in v2; an equal-lightness red button on grey took 14.7% of heat in v1 and 32.6% in v2. On my rebuilt stimulus v1 favoured the flat button, the opposite direction to the Section 8 anecdote, so the edge bias depends on the stimulus; the consistent finding is that v2 treats the two the same.

## 16. Motion module stress test and rebuild (added Oct 2026)

`motion_emd.py` (v1) was probed with synthetic clips with known ground truth (`game_motion_test.py`, `compare_motion.py`). It failed five ways; `motion_v2.py` fixes each:

| Flaw in v1 | Measured (v1 to v2) | Fix in v2 |
|---|---|---|
| Camera pan flagged as motion | 47.2% to 0.0% of pixels | Ego-motion compensation (LK + RANSAC similarity, guarded so a lone mover on a flat background is not mistaken for the camera) |
| Near-blind to vertical motion of horizontal edges | 194x to 1.0x (equal-length bars) | Bidirectional (x and y) correlators |
| Brightness inflated the signal | 2.63x to 1.00x at equal contrast | Local contrast normalisation (cf. contrast normalisation in fly motion vision, Drews et al. 2020) |
| Only one region returned | 3-mover recall 36% to 100% | Top-K regions with energy shares |
| Per-frame min-max normalisation let one bright mover erase the rest | noise clip: top region on target 81% to 93% | Fixed-scale, log-compressed energy; noise-calibrated threshold |
| (extra) single offset tuned to about 1 px/frame | v2 98.3% at 16 px/frame | 3-level pyramid (speed tuning doubles per level) |

Pan plus two bright fighters: fighters hold 99.5% of motion energy in v2 (79.2% in v1). Camera zoom plus a mover: 100% on target in both.

**Real footage** (`real_video_test.py`; OpenCV sample clip, scikit-video samples incl. Big Buck Bunny, (c) Blender Foundation, CC-BY):
- Static camera, walking pedestrians: agreement with a background-subtraction proxy (MOG2) AUC 0.812 to 0.995. The proxy is a movement signal, not human attention, and the CI treats frames as independent so it is optimistic.
- Handheld clip: v2 first flagged more than 20% of pixels in 8/249 frames (v1: 2/249). Fixed with a per-frame robust threshold, **which I tuned on that same clip**, so it is a development-set result. After: 0/249 (v1: 2/249). Big Buck Bunny: 0/131 vs v1 5/131.

**Regressions and limits found.** v2 misses very slow movers (below roughly 1.5 px per frame at 640 px width): on-target 100% to 93.2% on the slow-explore probe. A single global camera model cannot remove parallax. No human eye-tracking of VIDEO was used, so none of the motion results is a validation against attention.

